    {
      "id": "static-versus-continuous-batch",
      "title": "Static rows hold dead weight; continuous rows refill",
      "caption": "Static batching runs until the slowest sequence finishes. A request that stops after 200 tokens leaves its neighbours' finished rows in the batch, still read on every step, emitting nothing, so the batch runs at the speed of its slowest member. Continuous batching rebuilds the batch at every decode step: finished sequences retire, their slots refill, and the batch dimension changes. The figure uses six decode steps, one long request and two short neighbours; the step count is illustrative.",
      "alt": "Seven lanes across six decode steps. Static R1 decodes through step five and finishes at step six. Static R2 and R3 finish at step one, but their rows stay as dashed idle cells for the remaining steps. Continuous R1 decodes through step five and finishes at step six. Continuous R2 and R3 finish at step one and their slots are freed, shown as empty cells. Continuous R4 is admitted at step two and decodes to the end. Continuous lanes never show idle rows.",
